/-
Fmt/MustBeRefused/M2ProgLF.lean: every proof in this list is false and must be refused. These are
the 8 false proofs of the second toy at full parameters, each with the refusal that was expected of
it.

The second toy's full parameters are `toyPar` (Fmt/M2Types.lean). A block is one step of the
protocol. PSC is the sum-check (the head of Fmt/M2Dec.lean).

These files are test fixtures: proofs that every verifier must refuse. Test data: every file here is
false, or breaks a rule of the protocol, and must be refused. A verifier that accepts one of them is
broken. The data are made-up toy data: no model and no weights. Each was made by the toy's own
prover with one block cheating in the named way while all other blocks go on honestly. So each is
expected to pass every test of form, proof of work and Merkle opening, and to fail at the last block
of the proximity test. That each does so is not tested in this file. Fmt/M2EngineLFwe.lean,
Fmt/M2EngineLFwf.lean and Fmt/M2EngineLFwg.lean test that each passes the strict reader and is
refused by the decision, the two stages of a verifier that the head of Fmt/M2EngineLFwa.lean
explains; the outcome in these three files does not tell at which check the decision refuses.

Sources. This file was generated by a program from files in the archive
toy-files-made-by-the-cuts-tools.tgz (sha256
f97d6774317660e9033841ab36ce4e63e376ebeb25542cf6f1f92e8f61644aa1), which the programs (the C++
implementation that comes with this Lean code) made for the second toy (a chain of three links;
Fmt/M2Dec.lean says what it is). The data are made-up toy data: no model and no weights. Taken from
the archive: FALSE-PROOFS-every-verifier-must-refuse/links/full/links.cheat-X.proof for each name X,
and BOTH-VERIFIERS.tsv in that folder of the archive (the archive is not in this repository). The
bytes are in Fmt/MustBeRefused/M2ProgLFa.lean and the files beside it, one false proof each. The
generating program stops unless every file that the archive's own list of checksums (SUMS) names,
the lists among them, has the sha256 given there. The file imports no Mathlib. The false proofs'
parameters are those of the honest proof (the parameter bytes are `full` in Fmt/M2Theirs.lean, which
holds no proof).

1. What the file contains.
   mustBeRefused    For each false proof: its name, its bytes, and the number of the check at which
                    it was expected to fail.
                      4, the linear check of the last block of the proximity test;
                      5, that block's checks at the drawn positions.
                    That number is taken from the archive's lists. The list of what is expected says
                    of itself that
                      its column of expected refusals was written down before any of these proofs
                        was first made;
                      its column of reasons was mended afterwards for four names, whose first
                        wording a later reading had found too narrow.
                    An earlier archive of the same toy added that nine of the names had been made
                    once before, on an older state of the programs, with the same outcome. Only the
                    numbers are recorded here.
                    Checks 1 to 3: 1, the form; 2, proof of work; 3, a Merkle opening (`table`,
                    Fmt/M2ProgLFFiles.lean).

2. What is checked when the file compiles (#guard). All sizes (the checksums are tested in the files
   that hold the bytes).

3. Theorems. None.

4. What is not claimed.
   No theorem. The tests (#guard) are evaluations that Lean makes when the file compiles. They speak
   of these bytes and of nothing else.
   Beyond what this head reports above of Fmt/M2EngineLFwe.lean, Fmt/M2EngineLFwf.lean and
   Fmt/M2EngineLFwg.lean, nothing is said here about what any verifier in this development answers
   on these files; see Fmt/M2EngineLFwe.lean, Fmt/M2EngineLFwf.lean, Fmt/M2EngineLFwg.lean and
   Run/M2Engine*.lean.

Terms: lean/GLOSSARY.md.
-/
